[ti:Day 30]

[00:00.00]lime [laɪm]n. 酸橙；石灰 a. 含石灰的
[00:02.76]spinoff [ˈspɪnɒf]n. 副产品；附带的利益
[00:06.00]customary [ˈkʌstəməri]a. 习惯的；惯例的 例 It is both customary and expected for customers to tip waiters when going out to restaurants, bars, and coffee shops in America. 搭 customary law 习惯法
[00:24.48]shirk [ʃɜːk]v. 推卸或逃避(责任、义务等)；开小差 搭 shirk one's duty towards 逃避对…的责任
[00:31.56]appendix [əˈpendɪks]n. 附录；附属物；阑尾 例 For convenience, we have also provided a glossary in an appendix. 搭 appendix to sth. 某物的附属物
[00:44.40]automotive [ˌɔːtəˈməʊtɪv]a. 汽车的；自动的，机动的 例 Despite the obvious growing pains, working together is the best means of long-term success in the automotive industry.
[01:01.08]retention [rɪˈtenʃn]n. 保持；保留；扣留
[01:04.56]slippery [ˈslɪpəri]a. 滑的；滑动的；油滑的；不稳固的 搭 slippery customer 滑头的家伙 / slippery surface 光滑面
[01:13.68]itemise [ˈaɪtəmaɪz]vt. 逐条记载；分条细列；(在纳税的回 执上)列出税收扣除额 例 It is necessary to itemise a detailed and nationwide market access catalogue for private investment.
[01:29.76]brand [brænd]n. 商标，品牌；所有权标记
[01:32.76]paperwork [ˈpeɪpəwɜːk]n. 文书工作 例 She is the right person for the paperwork.
[01:40.32]reap [riːp]v. 获得；得到(报偿)
[01:42.84]rota [ˈrəʊtə]n. 值班表；花名册
[01:45.36]standardise [ˈstændədaɪz]vt. 使标准化 例 We will take measures to standardise the procedure of legal services.
[01:56.28]foodstuff [ˈfuːdstʌf]n. 食品；粮食 例 The government maintains strategic reserves of pork and salt among its major foodstuff resources. 搭 a shortage of basic foodstuffs 基本食物短缺
[02:13.68]augment [ɔːɡˈment]v. (在数量、尺寸、强度、效力等方面) 增加，增大，扩大 例 If prices remain high, more of the world's existing stock will augment supply. 搭 augment one's income 增加收入
[02:29.76]understanding [ˌʌndəˈstændɪŋ]n. 理解；谅解；非正式的协议 搭 come to an understanding 取得谅解，达成 共识 / on the understanding that 以…为条件； 如果
[02:42.24]barcode [ˈbɑːkəʊd]n. 条形码；条码技术 例 You can scan the barcode on an item and then can access the information of the product.
[02:54.12]acquire [əˈkwaɪə(r)]vt. 获得，得到 例 It is necessary to acquire a good knowledge of marketing.
[03:03.24]interface [ˈɪntəfeɪs]n. 接口；界面；交接处 例 The page interface is user-friendly and easy to operate. 搭 on-line user interface 联机用户接口
[03:16.08]advance [ədˈvɑːns]n. (价格等)增长；预付(款等)；前进； 进展 vt. 贷款；预付 vi. (价格等)上涨；改进 a. 预 先的 例 She asked the company director for an advance on a month's salary. 搭 in advance 提前，事先 / in advance of 在… 之前
[03:34.32]lateral [ˈlætərəl]a. 横向的；侧面的 例 There will be some lateral transfers for him in the company. 搭 lateral migration 侧向迁移 / lateral order distribution 侧序分布 / lateral thinking 水平思 考；横向思维
[03:52.56]fractious [ˈfrækʃəs]a. 易怒的；倔强的；难以对待的 例 The art of Belgian political leadership consists of bringing consensus to a broad and fractious coalition.
[04:07.68]opponent [əˈpəʊnənt]n. 对手，敌手；反对者
[04:11.28]medium-sized [ˈmiːdiəmsaɪzd]a. 中型的，中等大小的；普通型 的
[04:16.44]exotic [ɪɡˈzɒtɪk]a. 奇异的；外(国)来的；异国情调的 例 Exotic vocabulary can often be heard in daily life due to globalisation. 搭 exotic clothes 奇装异服 / exotic word 外来 词
[04:31.80]proprietary [prəˈpraɪətri]a. 所有人的；业主的；专用的；专 利的；专卖的 n. 所有人；业主；所有权
[04:38.88]wareroom [ˈweəruːm]n. 商品展销室；商品储藏室
[04:42.60]derive [dɪˈraɪv]v. 得到；源于 例 The world economy failed to derive much support from the continued weakness of the dollar. 搭 derive from 起源于
[04:56.16]pivotal [ˈpɪvətl]a. 关键的；枢轴的，中枢的 例 She plays a pivotal role in product design.
[05:04.08]cushion [ˈkʊʃn]n. 银行储蓄；缓冲物 v. 对…起缓冲作用 例 Unfortunately it turned out that banks still wanted to hold excess reserves as a cushion.
[05:17.16]furious [ˈfjʊəriəs]a. 激烈的；狂怒的；喧闹的 例 He gulped back the furious reply he wanted to make. 搭 be furious with sb. about sth. 因某事对某人 大发雷霆
[05:30.36]absorb [əbˈsɔːb]vt. 承担(费用)；兼并；吸收 例 The firms usually absorb the higher wage costs by raising prices of their commodities. 搭 be absorbed by 被…吞并 / be absorbed in 沉浸于
[05:46.80]sprint [sprɪnt]v. 疾跑；冲刺 n. 全速短跑；冲刺 例 Speed is essential in a sprint. 搭 in the sprint 在冲刺阶段
[05:55.68]amusement [əˈmjuːzmənt]n. 娱乐，消遣；娱乐活动，消遣方 式 例 Outdoor amusement will help you relax after a day's work at the offlce.
[06:07.56]allot [əˈlɒt]vt. 分配，分派；拨给 例 I allot a certain amount of time to analyse the historic data on stock each day. 搭 allot sth. to sb./sth. 把…分配给…派 allotment n. 配股；分派，分配；分配额(例： allotment of proflt 利润分配)
[06:27.24]formulate [ˈfɔːmjuleɪt]vt. 制定；配置；有系统地阐述；使 公式化 例 They must be qualifled to analyze, appraise and interpret economic data and government programs, as well as formulate and recommend national economic policy.
[06:48.60]spreadsheet [ˈspredʃiːt]n. 空白表格(用于会计等)；电子制表 软件；电子数据表
[06:54.48]noncommittal [ˌnɒnkəˈmɪtl]a. 态度不明朗的；不承担义务的 例 With regard to labour insurance，the Union President gave a noncommittal answer.
[07:07.68]fluffy [ˈflʌfi]a. 有绒毛的；蓬松的；松软的；肤浅的， 空洞的 例 It's a very fluffy kind of wool.
[07:15.24]acute [əˈkjuːt]a. 严重的；激烈的；敏锐的；(疾病)急性 的；尖锐的 例 The economic crisis in Europe has moved from an acute phase to a chronic one. 搭 acute sense 敏锐的感觉
[07:29.52]monopolise [məˈnɒpəlaɪz]vt. 垄断；获得专卖权 例 Our company has monopolised the mobile market for so many decades that we don't worry about competition f rom other companies.
[07:46.44]publicise [ˈpʌblɪsaɪz]vt. 宣传；推广
[07:49.92]fiscal [ˈfɪskl]a. 国库的；财政的；会计的 n. 印花税票 例 The accountants will audit the company's books at the end of the flscal year.
[08:01.68]payload [ˈpeɪləʊd]n. 有效载荷；工资负担；净载重量
[08:05.64]despatch [dɪˈspætʃ]n. (=dispatch) 差遣；差遣费；发货 v. 差遣；发货；发送 例 The despatch of railway transportation must be unifled and the construction of the overall network must be under integrated planning.
[08:25.44]deadlock [ˈdedlɒk]n. 僵局；停顿 vt. 停顿；相持不下 vi. 成僵局 例 Gold prices in China declined in December as the deadlock in the United States' fiscal cliff bargain added to the market uncertainty. 搭 break the deadlock 打破僵局
[08:46.32]malign [məˈlaɪn]vt. 诽谤；中伤 a. 恶意的；有害的；恶 性的 例 You malign a hard-working employee when you call him a lazy person.
[08:57.48]retaliatory [rɪˈtæliətri]a. 报复性的
[09:01.08]prominent [ˈprɒmɪnənt]a. 突出的；杰出的；显著的
[09:05.16]flawed [flɔːd]a. 有瑕疵的；有缺陷的 例 From my experience, at least 2/3 of all business plans we received are flawed.
[09:15.96]politicise [pəˈlɪtɪsaɪz]vt. 使政治化；使具有政治性 例 He raised objections at efforts to politicise the national army.
[09:27.12]fling [flɪŋ]vt. (用力地)扔，掷；使扑向；使投身；(气 愤地、猛然地)走动 n. 投，掷；尝试 例 You shouldn't fling away a chance like that. 搭 fling down a challenge 提出挑战 / fling oneself into 投身 / in full fling 莽撞地
[09:44.64]trounce [traʊns]vt. 痛击；严责，呵斥
[09:47.76]doldrums [ˈdɒldrəmz]n. 停滞，无进展 例 Affected by the global financial crisis, America remains in the economic doldrums. 搭 in the doldrums 意志消沉；萧条
[10:02.16]faulty [ˈfɔːlti]a. 有错误的；有缺点的；劣等的 例 To achieve the objective, we would change the former faulty understanding of development.
[10:15.12]category [ˈkætəɡəri]n. 种类；类别
[10:18.24]divisive [dɪˈvaɪsɪv]a. 造成分裂的，造成不和的 例 She believes that unemployment is socially divisive and is leading to the creation of an underclass.
[10:32.40]workaholic [ˌwɜːkəˈhɒlɪk]n. 工作狂 例 She really lives up to the reputation as a workaholic.
[10:41.40]triplicate [ˈtrɪplɪkət]a. 一式三份的 n. 一式三份
[10:45.60]payslip [ˈpeɪslɪp]n. 工资单
[10:48.24]partnership [ˈpɑːtnəʃɪp]n. 合伙；合伙企业；合作关系；合伙 契约
[10:53.28]unsustainable [ˌʌnsəˈsteɪnəbl]a. 无法支撑的；不可证实的；不 可持续的
[10:59.04]retrench [rɪˈtrentʃ]vt. 紧缩开支，节约经费；缩减；删 除，省略 vi. 节省，缩减开支 例 Two hundred workers were retrenched during the flnancial crisis.
[11:11.88]liberal [ˈlɪbərəl]n. 自由主义者 a. 自由的；慷慨的；宽大 的
[11:16.32]sidestep [ˈsaɪdstep]v. 回避，逃避 搭 sidestep responsibility 逃避责任
[11:22.68]commonplace [ˈkɒmənpleɪs]n. 老生常谈；司空见惯的事物；普 通的事物 a. 平凡的，陈腐的 例 A $99 extension of a commonplace device will be a no-brainer for a large set of media consumers.
[11:38.64]remit [rɪˈmɪt]vt. 免除；取消(债务等)；赦免；缓和，减 轻；汇款
[11:43.20]hotbed [ˈhɒtbed]n. 温床；滋生发展的环境(或地区) 例 As a hotbed of war, these islands are home to many sunken freighters, submarines and planes.
[11:56.40]vicarious [vɪˈkeəriəs]a. 代理的；代理人的；替代的；代用 的
[12:01.08]correspond [ˌkɒrəˈspɒnd]vi. 符合，一致；协调；通信 例 His design, for the most part, corresponds with the actual needs. 搭 correspond to 符合；满足 / correspond with sb. 与某人通信
[12:17.16]proliferation [prəˌlɪfəˈreɪʃn]n. 增大；扩散；激增 例 No one can stop mobile device proliferation.
[12:26.52]vertical [ˈvɜːtɪkl]a. 垂直的；纵向的；直立的 搭 vertical flle 直立式档案柜
[12:32.52]freelance [ˈfriːlɑːns]a. 自由职业的；特约的 n. 自由职业者 例 Finding freelance work can be tough, especially if you're just getting into the industry. 搭 freelance writer 自由撰稿人
[12:48.96]launch [lɔːntʃ]vt. 创办；发起；把…投放市场 n. 新产品 的推出 例 The company advocates all the salesmen to launch a big promotion for their new products. 搭 launch (out) into 开始从事，着手进行
[13:05.40]accredit [əˈkredɪt]vt. 相信，认可；把…归于；委任 例 He is accredited as being the flrst person to bring in the new concept of cost control. 搭 accredit sth. to sb./accredit sb. with sth. 把…归于某人
[13:23.40]haemorrhage [ˈhemərɪdʒ]n. 大出血；大量流失；大量的财产损 失 v. 损失财产 例 On further examination the true reason why the company haemorrhaged money for months will be found. 搭 cerebral haemorrhage 脑溢血 / internal haemorrhage 内出血
[13:44.64]heighten [ˈhaɪtn]vt. 加高；提高；增强 例 The advertising company is constantly looking for new techniques to heighten its surveillance of Internet users.
[13:59.64]outskirts [ˈaʊtskɜːts]n. 外围地区；郊区，市郊 例 The store is situated in the outskirts of the small town.
[14:09.36]vanguard [ˈvænɡɑːd]n. 前锋，先锋；领导者
[14:12.84]platform [ˈplætfɔːm]n. 平台；站台；(政党的)纲领；宣言
[14:17.28]vacant [ˈveɪkənt]a. 空着的；(职位等)空缺的；无人继承 的 例 The post of general manager fell vacant following his resignation.
[14:28.56]retrain [ˌriːˈtreɪn]vt. 再培训，再教育 例 She is currently retraining to become a real lawyer.
[14:37.44]revalue [ˌriːˈvæljuː]vt. 重新评价；对…重新估价 例 Such issues can provide new common ground between the US and Japan.
[14:48.36]putative [ˈpjuːtətɪv]a. 推定的，假定的；公认的
[14:52.32]invitation [ˌɪnvɪˈteɪʃn]n. 邀请；邀请书；招标单 例 We have received your invitation for Bid No. 315. 搭 at the invitation of sb. 受某人邀请
[15:04.68]wane [weɪn]n./v. 减少；衰落，衰退 例 Some traditional sections are beginning to wane amid soaring labour costs and outdated business models. 搭 on the wane 日益衰落，逐渐败落 / wane to the close 接近尾声
[15:22.80]adequate [ˈædɪkwət]a. 适当的；胜任的；充足的，足够的 搭 be adequate for 对…来说是足够的 / be adequate to do sth. 胜任某工作 / take adequate measures 采取适当措施
[15:35.76]tighten [ˈtaɪtn]v. (使)变紧，(使)绷紧；(钱)难以被贷到 例 Money has started to tighten due to the flnancial crisis. 搭 tighten up 加紧；使紧密
[15:48.00]economise [ɪˈkɒnəmaɪz]v. 节省，节约 例 Robots are used in some factories to economise on the cost of manpower.
